{"type": "answer", "run_id": "steady-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "steady", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q4", "request_id": "steady-20261110T183141Z-0006", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:34:03.951+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 142.729, "patience_s": 62.44, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 1343.0, "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "steady-20261110T183141Z-0006", "question": "Per Table 1, how does self-attention compare with recurrent layers on complexity per layer, sequential operations and maximum path length?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "Self‑attention layers have a per‑layer computational complexity of \\(O(n^{2}\\,d)\\), require only a constant number of sequential operations \\(O(1)\\), and have a maximum path length of \\(O(1)\\) between any two positions [S2].  \nRecurrent layers, in contrast, cost \\(O(n\\,d^{2})\\) per layer, need \\(O(n)\\) sequential operations, and their maximum path length also scales as \\(O(n)\\) [S2].  \nThus, self‑attention is computationally more expensive than recurrent layers only when the sequence length \\(n\\) exceeds the representation dimension \\(d\\), but it offers far greater parallelism (constant‑time sequential depth) and a dramatically shorter longest dependency path [S1][S2].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.6-7 §4", "pid": "attention:2400:0012", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p06-b011", "attention-p06-b012", "attention-p06-b013", "attention-p06-b014", "attention-p07-b001"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.5-6 §3.4", "pid": "attention:2400:0010", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p05-b017", "attention-p05-b018", "attention-p06-b001", "attention-p06-b002"]}, {"sid": "S3", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.7 §4", "pid": "attention:2400:0013", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p07-b002", "attention-p07-b003"]}], "n